A||||4||EdsB52||Engine removal tool||||1||
Z||000000||EdsB52||08-05-2000||03:20 AM||||With as much comprehensive information and knowledge that the members of this club have, and there are honestly some real geniuses, I think I'm one up on ya in one area! Tell me if someone's thought of this before. Eight years ago, when I bought my first Fiero with a thrown rod, I had to figure out a way to remove the engine without the aid of this club. I realized that the engine had to come out the bottom, so I drew up a tool and had a weld shop fab it for $50. It's simple. it consists of three parts; 2 ea rings with tabs welded, 1 ea rectangular bar with a tab welded on. I wish I had access to a floppy drive camera, as it would be much more clear.<P>The rings are made of flat stock steel about 1/8" to 3/16" thick. The I.D. of the rings should be a hair larger than the strut attach swells or raised area. The O.D. of the rings should be approx 6" more than the I.D. You then weld two tabs perpendicular to the plate. The exact proximity of these tabs depends upon the dimension of the tube that you select. Anyway, you then drill the three holes in these rings at the location used to secure your struts to the body. Obviously, the nuts and oval washers get removed and the newly fabricated rings get installed on the three studs protruding through the body, and then the nuts get installed. At this point, you get the tube that was pre-selected and place it between the tabs, drill a hole through the tabs and tube, and install bolts through the assy. The actuall order of fabrication and drilling will be a litle different to ensure conformance, but I think this should give a good overview.<P>This tool works awesomely, I have used it more than ten times, and it expedites R&R of the engine.<P>Use of the tool is obvious. Install tool, hook up cherry picker, raise rear end of car slightly, remove wheels and all wires and attachments. Lower rear of car onto legs of cherry picker. Remove craddle bolts. Raise rear of car as high as you can until cherry picker hoist arm almost touches rear taillights, install jack stands under indents by stub frame (you'll see 'em, they're perfectly located), and detach cherry picker hoist arm. At this point, the craddle is sitting on the cherry picker, so simply slide it out one of the corners of the car (it barely clears). I'm going to yank my motor in a couple months, so I'll try to get a camera and put my words into pic's. Trust me, this is an easy, safe way to yank the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||08-05-2000||04:40 AM||||are you talking about a bar that mounts to the strut towers.  and you lift the car from that bar?<P>I know GM made a bar that goes across the strut tower that held the engine up in the air while changing trans, oilpans. etc..||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||08-05-2000||10:30 AM||||I was thinking of building something just like that when I get ready to pull my motor. Do you unbolt the struts at the top and then bolt on your new plate?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000003||wkayl||08-05-2000||11:13 AM||||To raise the body of the engine cradle of an 84 I just removed the rear bumper to get access to the frame, attached a chain to the frame, and used a chain hoist attached to reinforced rafters, and just lifted it off.  I made a dolly out of 2x4s with casters to roll the cradle out.  I was not a member of the Forum at that time or I would have taken pictures.||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||08-05-2000||12:26 PM||||Yes fierospeeder, the bar does go accross the strut towers. Any form of this contraption will work. you can deviate from my design and have the same success. It just makes good sense to me to find a place to yank the car body up, when you have a vehicle that requires engine removal from the bottom. Exactly, that's where I originally stole that idea from, the maintenance manual showed a picture of that tool that held the engine up and I deviated from there.<P>Exactly Roy, you remove the three bolts at each strut tower and mount the "rings" on them. I don't care if you do it once in your life, it's worth building one of them to getthe job done. I don't see how people remove the engine safely by the manual. They show taking a 4x4 on a floor jack. What a gaggle****!<P>That sounds like a safe way to do it Wkayl, but very arduous in that you have to remove the bumper cover. That way sounds more safe than jacking the body up with a floor jack and 4x4 though.||||reg||1
